# Leahy, Fuzy & Grafe - Providing Home Care: A Textbook for Home Health Aides (Hartman Publishing).
# Chapter list: 7th edition (2025), from the publisher's sample contents (hartmanonline.com).
# Summaries cover each chapter's standard topics; they are written for this site, not copied from the book.

def ch(n, title, text, keys):
    return (n, title, [text], keys)

C = [
    ch(1, "Home Care and the Healthcare System", "Home health care lets clients receive care at home, which is usually less costly and more comfortable. Payers include Medicare (requires the client to be homebound and under a physician's plan of care), Medicaid, private insurance, and private pay. Agencies may be Medicare-certified.", ["Care at home", "Medicare: homebound + plan of care", "Medicaid and private pay", "Certified agencies"]),
    ch(2, "The Home Health Aide and the Care Team", "Home health aides (HHAs) assist with personal care, light housekeeping, and observing and reporting. They work under a supervisor (usually an RN) and follow the care plan. Federal rules require at least 75 hours of training, including 16 hours of supervised practical training.", ["Follow the care plan", "RN supervisor", "75 hours training", "Observe and report"]),
    ch(3, "Legal and Ethical Issues", "Clients have rights including privacy, dignity, and making choices. HHAs must keep information confidential (HIPAA), avoid accepting gifts or money, and report abuse or neglect. Types of abuse include physical, emotional, sexual, financial, and neglect.", ["Clients' rights", "HIPAA", "Don't accept gifts", "Report abuse"]),
    ch(4, "Communication and Diversity", "Communication includes verbal and nonverbal messages, listening, and adapting for impairments. HHAs report to the supervisor and document care. Respecting cultural and religious differences is essential. Combative behavior should not be taken personally; stay calm and report it.", ["Verbal and nonverbal", "Document care", "Respect diversity", "Stay calm with combative clients"]),
    ch(5, "Infection Prevention and Standard Precautions", "The chain of infection is broken by hand hygiene, Standard Precautions, PPE, and cleaning. Transmission-Based Precautions add measures for airborne, droplet, and contact diseases. Bloodborne diseases include HIV and hepatitis B. Report exposures immediately.", ["Hand hygiene", "Standard Precautions", "PPE", "Report exposures"]),
    ch(6, "Safety and Body Mechanics", "Good body mechanics prevent injury: wide stance, bend knees, keep objects close, avoid twisting. Common home accidents include falls, burns, poisoning, suffocation, and cuts. Fire safety includes smoke detectors and escape plans. HHAs should also follow car and personal safety guidelines.", ["Body mechanics", "Prevent falls and burns", "Fire safety", "Personal safety"]),
    ch(7, "Emergency Care and Disaster Preparation", "HHAs recognize emergencies, call 911, and give first aid for choking (abdominal thrusts), bleeding (direct pressure), shock, burns, fainting, seizures, and heart attack. Disaster plans prepare clients and aides for fires, storms, and evacuations.", ["Call 911", "Abdominal thrusts", "Direct pressure for bleeding", "Disaster plans"]),
    ch(8, "Physical, Psychological, and Social Health", "Basic human needs include physical, safety, love, and esteem needs. Holistic care considers the whole person. Illness affects clients and families emotionally; HHAs support adjustment and connect clients to community resources.", ["Basic needs", "Holistic care", "Family adjustment", "Community resources"]),
    ch(9, "Body Systems and Related Conditions", "HHAs learn normal function and common conditions of each body system: skin (pressure injuries), musculoskeletal (arthritis, fractures), nervous (stroke, Parkinson's), circulatory (heart failure, hypertension), respiratory (COPD), urinary (UTIs), digestive, endocrine (diabetes), and immune (HIV, cancer).", ["Pressure injuries", "Stroke and Parkinson's", "Diabetes", "COPD and heart failure"]),
    ch(10, "Confusion, Dementia, and Alzheimer Disease", "Dementia is a serious loss of mental abilities; Alzheimer's is the most common type. Care tips: keep routines, use simple communication, use validation, and manage behaviors like wandering, sundowning, and agitation calmly.", ["Alzheimer's most common", "Routines help", "Validation", "Sundowning"]),
    ch(11, "Human Development and Aging", "People develop through life stages from infancy to older adulthood. Normal aging brings changes like thinner skin, weaker bones, and slower reactions, but confusion and depression are not normal aging.", ["Life stages", "Normal aging changes", "Confusion is not normal", "Independence"]),
    ch(12, "Positioning, Transfers, and Ambulation", "HHAs reposition clients at least every 2 hours, use gait belts for transfers and walking, help with assistive devices (canes, walkers, crutches), and transfer clients to wheelchairs safely.", ["Reposition every 2 hours", "Gait belt", "Canes and walkers", "Safe transfers"]),
    ch(13, "Personal Care Skills", "Personal care includes bathing, shampooing, grooming, oral care, denture care, shaving, nail care, dressing (weak side first), and toileting. Encourage independence and protect privacy.", ["Dress weak side first", "Promote independence", "Protect privacy", "Oral and denture care"]),
    ch(14, "Core Healthcare Skills", "Core skills include vital signs, measuring intake and output, collecting specimens, caring for catheters, applying non-sterile dressings, and warm and cold applications. Report abnormal findings.", ["Vital signs", "I&O", "Specimens", "Catheter care"]),
    ch(15, "Medications and Technology in Home Care", "HHAs may assist with self-administered medications as allowed by state law and agency policy—reminding clients and reading labels—but generally don't give medications. The five rights are right client, drug, dose, route, and time. Home medical equipment includes oxygen (no smoking or flames).", ["Assist, don't administer", "Five rights", "Oxygen safety", "Home equipment"]),
    ch(16, "Rehabilitation and Restorative Care", "Rehabilitation helps clients regain function; restorative care maintains it. HHAs support range-of-motion exercises, adaptive devices, and independence in activities of daily living (ADLs).", ["Restore and maintain function", "ROM exercises", "Adaptive devices", "ADL independence"]),
    ch(17, "Clients with Disabilities", "Disabilities may be physical, developmental, or sensory. HHAs promote independence, use person-first language, and adapt care for vision or hearing impairment.", ["Person-first language", "Promote independence", "Adapt for sensory loss", "Respect abilities"]),
    ch(18, "Mental Health and Mental Health Disorders", "Mental health disorders include anxiety, depression, bipolar disorder, schizophrenia, and substance use disorders. HHAs observe and report changes, especially signs of suicide risk, and support treatment plans.", ["Observe mood changes", "Report suicide signs", "Support treatment", "Reduce stigma"]),
    ch(19, "New Mothers, Infants, and Children", "HHAs may help new mothers (watch for heavy bleeding, support breastfeeding) and infants (safe sleep on the back, bathing, umbilical cord care, feeding). Report signs of child abuse.", ["Safe sleep on back", "Cord care", "Postpartum bleeding", "Report child abuse"]),
    ch(20, "Dying, Death, and Hospice", "Hospice provides comfort care for terminally ill clients and supports families. HHAs respect advance directives and DNR orders, provide comfort measures, and recognize signs of approaching death. Grief stages (Kübler-Ross) help explain reactions.", ["Hospice comfort care", "Advance directives", "Signs of approaching death", "Grief stages"]),
    ch(21, "Clean, Safe, and Healthy Environments", "HHAs keep the home clean and safe: cleaning kitchens and bathrooms, doing laundry, disinfecting, and controlling pests. Use proper cleaning products and never mix bleach with ammonia.", ["Clean and disinfect", "Laundry", "Never mix bleach and ammonia", "Pest control"]),
    ch(22, "Clients' Nutritional Needs", "Good nutrition follows MyPlate. HHAs follow special diets (low-sodium, diabetic, modified consistency), assist with feeding, watch for swallowing problems, and encourage fluids.", ["MyPlate", "Special diets", "Swallowing precautions", "Encourage fluids"]),
    ch(23, "Meal Planning, Shopping, Preparation, and Storage", "HHAs plan meals within budget and diet orders, shop wisely (lists, unit pricing, labels), prepare food safely, and store it properly to prevent foodborne illness.", ["Meal planning", "Shopping lists", "Food safety", "Proper storage"]),
    ch(24, "Managing Time, Energy, and Money", "HHAs organize work with schedules and priorities, save energy with good work habits, and handle client money honestly with receipts, following agency policy.", ["Prioritize tasks", "Work efficiently", "Keep receipts", "Honest money handling"]),
    ch(25, "Caring for Yourself and Your Career", "HHAs manage stress, maintain health, and grow their careers through continuing education (12 hours yearly), professionalism, and good job-seeking skills.", ["Manage stress", "12 hours CE yearly", "Professionalism", "Career growth"]),
]

